{"name":"Royal Consort","hex":"#2e5686","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"consort","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The spouse of a monarch.","A husband, wife, companion or partner."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of a title, by virtue of one's (living) spouse; often contrasted with regnant and dowager"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/consort"}],"entries":[{"title":"Royal consort","blocks":[{"text":"A royal consort is a person granted official status through an intimate relationship, often through marriage or concubinage, with a monarch. The term originates from the Latin consors, meaning \"partner\", and can be used in everyday English as a synonym for that word, and as a verb meaning \"to associate\". As it pertains to royalty, the term \"has its roots in seventeenth-century vocabulary in both New England and England\", where it was initially used to mean simply a spouse. It has since been extended to encompass similar relationships with other significant figures, such as a head of state."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_consort","wikidata":"Q3273157"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"royal":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Pvanp7-royal.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/c1/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Pvanp7-royal.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Pvanp7-royal.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\""